= Solution
{parent=a}

A standard one-dimensional <Brownian motion> is a real-valued <stochastic process> $(B_t)_{t\geq0}$ satisfying $B_0=0$ <almost surely>, having <continuous paths> <almost surely>, and having <independent increments> with
$$
\boxed{B_t-B_s\sim N(0,t-s)\qquad(0\leq s<t).}
$$
Here $N(0,t-s)$ is the <normal distribution> with zero <expected value> and <variance> $t-s$. Thus the increments are also <stationary increments>.

If <Brownian motion> is defined relative to a specified <filtration> $(\mathcal F_t)$, additionally require that it is an <adapted process> and that $B_t-B_s$ is <independence>[independent] of $\mathcal F_s$. For its <natural filtration>, this follows from <independent increments>. **These are the defining properties of standard <Brownian motion>.**

= b
{parent=4}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

For any finite list $t_1,\ldots,t_k\in[0,1]$, the <random vector> $(U_{t_1},\ldots,U_{t_k},B_1)$ has a <multivariate normal distribution>, since it is a linear image of a vector of <Brownian motion> coordinates. The <Brownian covariance kernel> and <linearity of covariance> give
$$
\operatorname{Cov}(U_t,B_1)=\operatorname{Cov}(B_t,B_1)-t\operatorname{Var}(B_1)=t-t=0.
$$
The <independence of uncorrelated jointly normal variables> implies that $(U_{t_1},\ldots,U_{t_k})$ is <independence>[independent] of $B_1$. Extending from finite-coordinate cylinder <events> by <independence extended from generating pi-systems> proves
$$
\boxed{\sigma(U_t:0\leq t\leq1)\ \text{is independent of}\ \sigma(B_1).}
$$
Thus **the <Brownian bridge> is <independence>[independent] of its endpoint**. Its <covariance kernel> is $\operatorname{Cov}(U_s,U_t)=\min(s,t)-st$, confirming that $U$ is the standard <Brownian bridge>. This is the <Brownian bridge independence from its endpoint> property.

= c
{parent=4}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=c}

Put $D_t=B_t-B'_t$. By <independence> of the two <Brownian motions>, $D_t/\sqrt2$ is a standard <Brownian motion>: its <independent increments> have the <normal distribution> with <variance> equal to elapsed time. The <stopping time> $\tau$ is the first time $D$ hits $-1$ or $3$.

To justify finiteness without presupposing the desired <hitting probability>, each unit-time increment of $D$ has the <normal distribution> $N(0,2)$. An increment of absolute value greater than $4$ forces exit from $[-1,3]$ if the path was inside at its start. These <independent increments> give $\mathbb P(\tau>n)\leq(1-p)^n$, where $p=\mathbb P(|N(0,2)|>4)>0$. Hence $\tau<\infty$ <almost surely>.

The <stopped martingale> $D_{t\wedge\tau}$ lies in $[-1,3]$. The <optional stopping theorem> at $t\wedge\tau$ gives $\mathbb ED_{t\wedge\tau}=0$, and the <bounded convergence theorem> gives $\mathbb ED_\tau=0$. Writing $q=\mathbb P(D_\tau=-1)$, path <continuity> gives $0=-q+3(1-q)$. Therefore, as in <Brownian exit from an interval>,
$$
\boxed{\mathbb P(B_\tau=B'_\tau-1)=\frac34.}
$$

= d
{parent=4}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=d}

Fix an integer $k\geq1$ and consider the <independent increments>
$$
\Delta_{j,k}=B_{(j+1)/k}-B_{j/k},\qquad j=0,1,2,\ldots.
$$
For fixed $k$ these are <independent and identically distributed random variables> with the <normal distribution> $N(0,1/k)$, so $p_k=\mathbb P(|\Delta_{j,k}|>1)>0$. The second <Borel-Cantelli lemma> gives $|\Delta_{j,k}|>1$ for infinitely many $j$, <almost surely>. Intersecting these probability-one <events> over the countably many $k$ preserves probability one.

On any path in this intersection, <uniform continuity> on $[0,\infty)$ would give some $\delta>0$ such that $|B_t-B_s|<1$ whenever $|t-s|<\delta$. Choosing $k$ with $1/k<\delta$ contradicts the existence of an increment $|\Delta_{j,k}|>1$. Therefore <Brownian paths are not uniformly continuous on the half-line>:
$$
\boxed{\mathbb P\bigl(B\text{ is uniformly continuous on }[0,\infty)\bigr)=0.}
$$
The event is measurable: for <continuous paths>, <uniform continuity> can be tested by countably many pairs of nonnegative <rational numbers> and tolerances $1/m$. **<Brownian motion> has <uniform continuity> on every compact time interval, but <almost surely> fails it on the whole half-line.**

